  "judge_reasoning": "# Evaluation\n\n## Task Analysis\nThe task asks two questions:\n1. For Non-English submissions, is a multi-language abstract required?\n2. If yes, what is the separator between the multiple abstracts?\n\n## Screenshot Analysis\nThe screenshots show arXiv help pages (both the archived FAQ page and the current policy page) regarding \"Submissions in Languages other than English, and Multiple Language Submissions\":\n\n**Question 1 verification:** The page clearly states: *\"We accept submissions in languages other than English, but do require that all submissions have an English version of the abstract. The principal reason for including an English abstract is to allow effective search and keyword extraction over all submissions in arXiv.\"* — This confirms that **yes**, a multi-language abstract (including an English version) is required.\n\n**Question 2 verification:** Under \"Preparation of a Multi-lingual Abstract,\" the page shows the format:\n```\nEnglish version of abstract for your important paper.\n\n-----\n\nOther language version of abstract for your important paper.\n```\nAnd explicitly notes: *\"Note the blank lines before and after the separator (-----).\"* — This confirms the separator is a line of dashes `-----`.\n\n## Response Analysis\nThe result response states:\n- \"Yes. For non-English submissions, arXiv requires a multi-language abstract: all submissions must have an English version of the abstract.\" ✓ Matches the screenshot content.\n- \"The separator between the multiple abstracts is a line of dashes '-----' (with blank lines before and after the separator).\" ✓ Matches the screenshot content exactly.\n\nBoth parts of the question are answered correctly and are fully supported by the visual evidence in the screenshots.\n\n## Verdict\n**SUCCESS**",
